.TH LEGBAR 1 "September 2026" "legbar 0.3.10" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
legbar \- one screen for the whole fleet: live agent sessions beside GitHub CI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B legbar
[\fB\-1\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-git\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-ci\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-color\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-auto\-install\fR]
[\fB\-i\fR \fISECS\fR]
[\fB\-\-waiting\-alert\fR \fIMINUTES\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B legbar
is a full-screen curses view that puts both fleet lanes on one canvas. The
SESSIONS pane lists every live agent on the machine; the CI / PRS pane lists
GitHub Actions runs and open pull requests across every clone, with failures
pinned so a red build cannot scroll away.
.PP
Both panes are drawn from a single discovery layer,
.BR henhouse ,
so the two views cannot disagree about what is running.
.B henhouse.py
is a working CLI in its own right (\fBpython henhouse.py\fR).
.PP
Claude Code sessions are joined by pid against the live-session markers, with
model, context burn and status read from the session's own JSONL transcript.
Cursor agents appear too, marked by a
.BR cu-
prefix on the session name (Claude Code sessions take
.BR cc- ).
Their liveness is inferred rather than probed \- Cursor writes no pid to join
against, so a row means \fIthis transcript moved recently\fR, which is a weaker
signal and is labelled as one.
.PP
Sessions driven through a local model gateway (LiteLLM/Ollama aliases such as
\fIreef-coder\fR or \fIgemma-32k\fR) are labelled \fB(local)\fR, render a
lowercase model code, and their burn is reported as a separate \fIlocal\fR
header figure rather than counted in \fIheld\fR \- local lanes cost nothing
against a paid plan. An alias that encodes its pinned context in its name
(\fIgemma-32k\fR) gets that as the context-bar denominator; otherwise the
percentage is omitted rather than computed against a window the alias never
had.
.PP
Read-only by construction: legbar reads transcripts and registries and runs
.BR gh
and read-only git plumbing. It never writes to a tree, a registry or a
session. Every data source is optional and degrades to a labelled gap rather
than an error \- an empty fleet renders an empty fleet, which is exactly the
case someone is looking at when they are debugging why nothing is running.
.PP
The output speaks one of two glyph dialects, chosen by the terminal at
startup: an interactive UTF-8 stdout gets rounded pane frames and Unicode
status glyphs. On Windows the encoding alone cannot decide it (every console
reports utf-8 since PEP 528), so Windows Terminal \- \fBWT_SESSION\fR in the
environment \- is the signal; a bare console, where block drawing mojibakes,
keeps ASCII, as does every pipe-safe surface (\fB\-\-once\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR,
a redirect). \fB\-\-ascii\fR or \fBLEGBAR_ASCII=1\fR forces the fallback;
\fBLEGBAR_UNICODE=1\fR forces the Unicode tier on a Windows console known to
draw it. The context bar is ASCII in both dialects.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-once
Render one frame to stdout and exit. For pipes, CI logs and screenshots.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the joined state as JSON and exit, for piping somewhere else. The
object's first key is
.BR version ,
so a consumer can tell which schema it is reading without shelling out to
\fB\-\-version\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-git
Skip the per-tree git probes. Toggle at runtime with \fBg\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ci
Skip the gh sweep entirely. Useful offline, or when the API is slow.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour in the full-screen view. \fBNO_COLOR\fR does the same.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Force the ASCII glyph dialect even on a UTF-8 terminal.
\fBLEGBAR_ASCII=1\fR does the same. Pipes, \fB\-\-once\fR and \fB\-\-json\fR
are always ASCII regardless, and so is a Windows console outside Windows
Terminal unless \fBLEGBAR_UNICODE=1\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-install
Windows only. When the full-screen view finds no
.B curses
module, legbar normally offers (one interactive y/N prompt) to run
\fBpip install windows-curses\fR against its own interpreter. This flag
suppresses the offer and prints the manual command instead. The prompt never
appears without a terminal on stdin, and \fB\-\-once\fR/\fB\-\-json\fR never
need curses at all.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fISECS\fR
Seconds between refreshes in the full-screen view (default 5).
.TP
.B \-\-waiting\-alert " " \fIMINUTES\fR
How long a conversation must sit unanswered before
.B NEEDS YOU
marks it with \fB!\fR and colours it (default 20). A younger wait is still
listed, just quiet \- a session waiting on you is the normal resting state of a
fleet, and flagging every one of them on sight teaches the eye to skip the
flag. \fB0\fR flags every wait. Contested trees are exempt and are loud from
the first frame.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B q
quit. Esc is deliberately ignored: on Windows PDCurses can deliver a false Esc
and would otherwise dump you out of the view.
.TP
.B r
refresh everything now
.TP
.B g
toggle the per-tree git probes
.TP
.B ?
help: what the symbols mean (\fB!!\fR, the context bar, the git cell), then
the keys. Any key closes it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every override is \fBLEGBAR_*\fR. The older
.BR roost ,
.BR leghorn
and
.BR ccwork
variable names are still honoured so an existing install keeps whatever it had
configured; the new name wins when both are set.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_REPOS_ROOT
Directory the clones live under (default ~/GitHub). The CI pane asks gh about
the ones with a github.com origin. Legacy: \fBLEGHORN_ROOT\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_SESSIONS_DIR
Claude Code's live-session markers, one <pid>.json per session (default
~/.claude/sessions). Legacy: \fBCCWORK_SESSIONS_DIR\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_PROJECTS_DIR
Claude Code's JSONL transcripts, keyed by mangled cwd (default
~/.claude/projects). Legacy: \fBCLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_STATE_DIR
Claims registry directory (default ~/Claude/worktrees). \fBregistry.json\fR
inside it feeds contested-tree detection and claimed-task labels. Legacy:
\fBCCWORK_STATE_DIR\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_CURSOR_HOME
Cursor's agent transcripts (default ~/.cursor). Legacy:
\fBCURSOR_AGENT_HOME\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_CURSOR_MAX_IDLE_SECS
How far back a Cursor transcript still counts as a live agent (default 86400).
Legacy: \fBROOST_CURSOR_MAX_IDLE_SECS\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_CURSOR_STATE_DB
Cursor's global SQLite index (\fBstate.vscdb\fR). When present, henhouse reads
\fBcomposerHeaders\fR for CTX% and composer names. Platform default under the
Cursor user data dir. Legacy: \fBROOST_CURSOR_STATE_DB\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_BACKENDS
Which discovery lanes run at all (default \fBclaude,cursor\fR). Legacy:
\fBROOST_BACKENDS\fR.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_ASCII
Any non-empty value forces the ASCII glyph dialect, like \fB\-\-ascii\fR.
Wins over \fBLEGBAR_UNICODE\fR when both are set.
.TP
.B LEGBAR_UNICODE
Any non-empty value allows the Unicode dialect on a Windows console that is
not Windows Terminal (no \fBWT_SESSION\fR) \- for a conhost running
\fBchcp 65001\fR with a Unicode font, or ConEmu. The stdout must still be an
interactive UTF-8 tty; pipes stay ASCII.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gh (1),
.BR git (1)
